# Universal Application Security & STRIDE Threat Modeling

This skill provides a comprehensive security engineering framework for desktop, web, cloud, and mobile applications, ensuring end-to-end defense-in-depth across the entire software lifecycle.

---

## 1. The STRIDE Threat Modeling Methodology

Evaluate every subsystem, endpoint, and IPC interface against the 6 fundamental threat categories:

| STRIDE Threat | Security Property | Common Vulnerability Vector | Defense-in-Depth Control |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **S**poofing | Authenticity | Forged session cookies, fake client IDs, impersonated JWT tokens | Cryptographic signatures (Ed25519, ES256), mutual TLS (mTLS), strict token validation |
| **T**ampering | Integrity | Memory modification, parameter tampering, MITM payload modification | Constant-time HMAC-SHA256, memory checksumming, TLS 1.3 certificate pinning |
| **R**epudiation | Non-repudiation | Untraceable actions, disabled or spoofed logs | Immutable write-ahead audit logs, cryptographic signing of administrative events |
| **I**nformation Disclosure | Confidentiality | Leaked API keys, unmasked PII, memory heap dumps, error stack traces | AES-256-GCM encryption at rest, secure zeroing of secrets, generic client errors |
| **D**enial of Service | Availability | Algorithmic complexity attacks (ReDoS), unbounded memory allocations, socket floods | Token bucket rate limiting, strict JSON/payload size bounds, streaming parsers |
| **E**levation of Privilege | Authorization | IDOR, path traversal (`../../etc/passwd`), DLL hijacking | Least privilege, chroot/namespaces, strict RBAC/ABAC role checks, canonicalized paths |

